              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "radiodense and are readily visualized. These",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "objects most commonly lodge at points of",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "minimal esophageal distension, including the",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "thoracic inlet, base of the heart, and",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "esophageal hiatus.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Esophageal distension with air may be",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "visualized cranial to the foreign body.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Retained air in the esophagus is not always",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "associated with esophageal foreign bodies.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "A contrast esophagram or videofluoroscopy",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "is required to identify radiolucent objects. If",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "perforation is suspected, use an aqueous",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "organic iodide contrast agent for imaging",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "studies.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Air and/or fluid in the mediastinum or",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "pleural space suggests esophageal perforation;",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "depending on severity, this can be an",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "indication for surgery versus esophagoscopy.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pulmonary infiltrates suggest aspiration",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": "Thoracic Radiography"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "pneumonia.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2,
