    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "GENERAL": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "GENERAL",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE",
          "canonical_heading": "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Emergencies—treat as inpatient and perform endoscopy as soon as possible after diagnosis.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "If endoscopic retrieval of the foreign body succeedsandesophageal damage is minimal, the patient may be discharged the same day.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Foreign bodies which cannot be removed through the mouth may be pushed aborally into the stomach.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "NURSING CARE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "NURSING CARE",
          "canonical_heading": "NURSING CARE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "If the procedure to remove the foreign body is atraumatic and the esophagus has sustained minimal damage, no special aftercare is needed.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Severe mucosal trauma may require placing a gastrostomy tube for enteral nutritional support during esophageal healing. Fluid therapy may also be required to maintain normal hydration status during periods of prolonged esophageal rest.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "ACTIVITY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "ACTIVITY",
          "canonical_heading": "ACTIVITY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The patient may resume normal activity after a foreign body has been routinely removed.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIET": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIET",
          "canonical_heading": "DIET",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "No change needed other than, perhaps, altering the food to a more liquid consistency.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Feeding should be withheld 12–24 hours following foreign body removal.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "CLIENT EDUCATION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CLIENT EDUCATION",
          "canonical_heading": "CLIENT EDUCATION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Discuss the possibility of complications and repeat offenders.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Surgery is indicated when endoscopy fails to retrieve the foreign body; when endoscopy enables advancement of the object into the gastric lumen but it is too large to pass through the gastrointestinal tract; or when a large esophageal perforation or area of necrosis requires resection.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The short-term prognosis for esophageal surgery is dogs and cats is favorable (~90%).",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "It is often less traumatic to advance a bone foreign body into the stomach than to attempt retrieval transorally via endoscopy. Gastrostomy, if required, may then be performed.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Most bone foreign bodies can be safely left to dissolve in the stomach without need for surgical removal. Nondigestible foreign objects (wood, metal, plastic) passed into the stomach may need to be removed surgically.",
              "pdf_page": 547,
              "printed_page": 465,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
